  "narrative": "**Narrative**\n\nThe RaMP enrichment analysis identified **Eicosanoid Synthesis** (WikiPathways WP167) as the dominant affected pathway (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 1250.95), with five of the eight input metabolites mapping to it: arachidonic acid (C00219), leukotriene A4 (C00909), 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂ (C14717), 5-HETE (C04805), and 15-HETE (C04742). A secondary enriched pathway, the **Selenium Micronutrient Network** (WP15; FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 365.14), additionally captured L-cysteine (C00097) and L-cystathionine (C02291), connecting the sulfur-amino-acid and eicosanoid subsystems.\n\nThe primary drivers of the eicosanoid signal are **arachidonic acid** and **leukotriene A4**. KEGG graph traversal confirms that arachidonic acid is upstream of leukotriene A4, proceeding via the 5-hydroperoxy intermediate (C05356) in two enzymatic steps (5-lipoxygenase pathway). This positions arachidonic acid as the substrate entry point driving the leukotriene branch.\n\nDownstream of leukotriene A4 lie the hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acids (5-HETE and 15-HETE) and the J-series prostaglandin 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂, all of which are confirmed members of the same eicosanoid synthesis network. These metabolites are established pro-inflammatory and pro-resolution lipid mediators whose simultaneous elevation is consistent with an acute inflammatory or signaling cascade.\n\nThe sulfur-amino-acid axis (L-cysteine → L-cystathionine) was independently enriched via the selenium micronutrient network. Cysteine is a known precursor for glutathione synthesis and for the transsulfuration pathway generating hydrogen sulfide, both of which intersect with oxidative-stress responses that parallel eicosanoid activation.\n\nMercaptopurine (C02380), while not captured in the top-ranked pathways, is a purine analog whose metabolism via hypoxanthine/guanine phosphoribosyltransferase intersects with purine salvage and can indirectly influence redox balance relevant to the observed oxidative/inflammatory signature. Its presence may reflect a treatment-specific effect or secondary perturbation.\n\nIn summary, the treatment group is characterised by coordinated up-regulation of the arachidonic-acid–derived eicosanoid cascade and the cysteine–cystathionine transsulfuration axis, suggesting an inflammatory or immune-activation phenotype with concurrent perturbation of sulfur-amino-acid metabolism. The directional arachidonic acid → leukotriene A4 → HETEs/prostaglandin axis is computationally verified via KEGG reaction graph traversal.",
